builds/agent-command/slice.out:946:description: Single-pass, read-only code review of the current branch's committed diff for correctness — logic bugs, regressions, edge cases, error handling, data integrity. Runs the shared review engine (review.mjs), returns structured findings by severity, fixes every HIGH and MEDIUM without asking, defers what the one fix check leaves, and reports once. The script never fixes; no loop.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:963:Context comes from files: the orchestrator's plan via --plan <plan.md> or REVIEW_PLAN, the builder's adjacent log.md if present, and already-decided findings from the review log. Inside a build loop, pass the plan; a missing plan file refuses. The optional intent string supplements those files. Decided stays decided: same-branch, same-repository rejections arrive as "already decided; don't re-raise without new evidence".
builds/agent-command/slice.out:966:**Who reviews.** The `reviewer` role in ACS (Models → Roles; `model-id --role reviewer --json`) is the pool, each family at its own flavor effort. A family's CLI follows its model id (`claude-…` → claude, `gpt-…` → codex, `grok-…` → grok); families whose CLI is not installed are skipped and one line names them. The run stops with a message naming the fix — never trying another vendor — when `model-id` is missing (`acs install-model-id`), the role is unset (you choose it at install: `acs packs install review-gate --set-role reviewer=<family>[,…]`) or missing, no family in it has its CLI, or a named reviewer has no CLI.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:972:**Grok runs.** A grok review runs with an empty temporary HOME and the real GROK_HOME, so nothing grok borrows from `~/.claude` or `~/.cursor` (plugin MCP servers, hooks) starts, and its login is the usual one. It is refused while grok would still start an MCP server, run the project's hooks, or run in leader mode (set in any grok config layer or by macOS device management; a layer that can't be read counts as on). `grokMcpAllow` lists servers from grok's own `config.toml` that may start anyway (their tools stay denied): operator-only, in the home config or `REVIEW_GROK_MCP_ALLOW`, never a tracked repo config, and empty by default; ask-model reads the same key from the home config.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:985:The slices together must cover every file of the diff: the push gate and `--summary` accept them only then, and a missing slice blocks the push and names the uncovered files. Coverage is by content, so a late commit needs only its own files reviewed as one more slice, closed at the tip; the slices closed earlier still count.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:987:**What the push gate accepts.** A changed file is covered when its exact state at the pushed tip (content and mode, or deleted) was seen by a complete close or a clean fix check (one with no finding that needs a status), logged in this repository at a commit the tip descends from. A review on another branch or in another clone covers nothing. Content already on the default branch needs no coverage: the gate trusts the commit that `refs/remotes/origin/HEAD` names (a symbolic ref to an existing `refs/remotes/origin/<branch>`, read locally, never set by the gate, never from `REVIEW_BASE` or config `base`), and only for a push to `origin`; any other remote, or a missing, detached or dangling origin/HEAD, keeps the full rule. A file whose diff the 120k cap cut is not covered, and a file changed on both sides of a merge needs a review that saw the merge. `builds/` files need no coverage. The tip also needs one complete close at it (or followed only by `builds/` commits), so the tests ran there. A refusal lists the uncovered files: review just those with `REVIEW_PATHS` and close that slice at the tip. **Update a reviewed branch by merging the default branch into it, never by rebasing** (a rebase needs coverage again). After a clean merge, re-close the existing review at the merge tip: the tests run there and no model is called. A file changed on both sides of the merge, conflict or not, and any file edited inside the merge commit itself, gets a slice review at the tip (`REVIEW_PATHS` = the files the refusal names) closed there; the re-close then refuses (with a fix check) or goes partial (without one) naming those same files, which is expected, and the slice's close is the one the push needs. Generated dependency lockfiles (package-lock.json, yarn.lock, pnpm-lock.yaml, Cargo.lock, go.sum and the others the gate lists, by exact file name in any folder) are exempt by default; the refusal and `--summary` name every exempt file as not reviewed, so the PR shows them. The operator can list more files in `coverageExempt` (globs, like `riskPaths`) or turn the defaults off with `"coverageExemptDefaults": false`: in the home config (`coverageExempt` also through `REVIEW_COVERAGE_EXEMPT`) or an untracked repo config, never a tracked one. A tracked repo config never drops these home-config values either: the home file's `coverageExempt`, `coverageExemptDefaults`, `grokMcpAllow`, `verifyCmd`, `classifier` and `base` stay in force over it. Agents don't add to the list on their own. A refusal for any other file whose diff alone is over the cap goes in the PR as a blocker for the operator, never as a shell command for them to paste.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:997:- **Pre-existing findings.** A reviewer marks `preExisting: true` when the defect exists at the base and this branch neither introduced it nor made it newly reachable; the script then records `inDiff`: whether any of its places is within 3 lines of a line the branch changed (the merge-base review diff). `preExisting: true` with `inDiff: false` is pre-existing: it never blocks, needs no status, is never deferred, and `--summary` lists it with its score under "Pre-existing (not introduced here)". A protected one is listed first as "Pre-existing HIGH" and makes the merge tier HIGH (no agent merge past a known exploit) without blocking the close. With `inDiff` true or null the claim is ignored and the finding blocks as usual. Original review findings only; a fix-check finding is never pre-existing.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:1000:**No confirmation (rule 10).** Send every HIGH and MEDIUM straight to the fix round, or dispute it with proof; never ask which to fix. A finding that is really a product choice (bug or feature?) gets the safe fix by default and is named in the report so the operator can reverse it. LOWs are filed. **One report, after the fix round and the fix check:** what was fixed, what was deferred and what was filed, each with its numbers (rule 11), plus any product-choice fix the operator may reverse; fixing the filed items is optional. Clean reviews still need --close and tests; a clean report alone is not done.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:1004:Fix the real cause directly (you have the diff loaded; do NOT re-spawn a model to fix). Keep fixes minimal and faithful; never suppress or paper over. Then run the repo's linter/formatter on the changed files and commit before --close, never afterward. Fixes never get a full re-review. Every non-filed finding needs a fixed or rejected status; unknown keys and any severity field refuse:
builds/agent-command/slice.out:1007:{"f1":{"status":"fixed"},"f2":{"status":"rejected","reason":"<why>","evidence":"<file:line or test>","refutedBy":{"review":"<prior review id>","finding":"<prior finding id>"}}}
builds/agent-command/slice.out:1010:After the fix check, its x… findings (and a Grade recheck's r… findings, or a criterion still failing its recheck) may instead be deferred: `{"x1":{"status":"deferred","reason":"<why>","fix":"<fix idea>"}}`. The reason is required; any severity or score field refuses; an original finding can't be deferred.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:1012:Label what each fixed or rejected finding turned out to be with `response`: on `fixed`, `"real"` or `"unimportant"` (fixed though not worth it; a fix without a label is logged as real); on `rejected`, `"wrong"` (not a defect) or `"intentional"` (the behavior is meant), required on every rejection, e.g. `{"f2":{"status":"rejected","reason":"<why>","evidence":"<file:line>","response":"intentional"}}`. A label never replaces the REFUTED verdict or waiver a blocking rejection needs. `node "$SCRIPT" --noise-report [--since <date>] [--all]` counts, per reviewer family and severity band, real / unimportant / wrong / intentional / unlabeled / deferred / filed / pre-existing findings from each review's latest close (this repository unless `--all`); legacy statuses count as unlabeled.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:1022:Close runs the whole test suite using verifyCmd from .claude/review-config.json or --test-cmd. If there are no tests, pass --no-tests "<reason>"; it is logged and appears in the summary. Only when close requires it, the orchestrator runs --fix-check <review-id> once. Fix, dispute or defer its x1… findings, close, then a hard stop, with no further model pass. Config `fixCheck`: `"risk"` (default: a fix touching tests, verify scripts or riskPaths needs a fix check) | `"always"` (the risk rule, plus any other code fix after the review; other docs, static, coverage-exempt and `builds/` files never need one, so it is never looser than "risk"; for a Grade, its recheck counts as the check when nothing either rule counts changed after it). The home config is the floor: a repo file, `REVIEW_CONFIG` or `REVIEW_FIX_CHECK` may raise it, never lower it. Fixes land before the fix check: commits after it may touch only `builds/` and test files, and close still runs the full tests. A file the fix check saw is in scope at close, so a fix to a sibling file is fine once the fix check has seen it. Close counts a changed file as fixed only when it equals, exactly, the path the script recorded when it checked that finding's quote; a finding with no recorded path (unlocated, ambiguous between a/ or b/ and the bare path, a Grade finding, or one logged before paths were recorded) covers no file. A rename counts as a delete plus an add, so the renamed path is never covered by a finding. A fixed finding covers its whole file, not only the lines it cited. A complete close covers what it saw at HEAD (see what the push gate accepts, above); a partial close lists outOfScope files and covers nothing. Use refutedBy for an already-decided finding rather than reopening its dispute without new evidence. Put --summary output in the PR body. --log-outcome is optional telemetry only; it never closes a review.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:1027:2. First round: every HIGH and MEDIUM from the original review is fixed, or rejected with a REFUTED verdict from a different model (`--dispute`). Nothing is deferred in this round; LOWs are filed automatically.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:1028:3. One fix round, then one fix check. What the fix check finds (`x…`, any severity) may be deferred: `{"x1":{"status":"deferred","reason":"<why>","fix":"<fix idea>"}}`. The reason is required, `fix` is optional (the reviewer's suggestion shows otherwise), and the reviewer's score stands. The close then completes; there is no second round. One exception, in this flow rather than in the gate: when the fix check raises a HIGH in the fix itself (`inFix: true`), defer it at close (the close completes), fix it in one more commit, then review that commit's files as one slice (`REVIEW_PATHS=<those files>`) and close the slice at the tip; whatever that slice's own fix check finds is deferred. Hard stop: no third pass.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:1029:4. Grade: a criterion still failing after one fix round and its one recheck may be deferred as "criterion not met" with the same status shape, and so may the recheck's own `r…` findings. It never silently passes.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:1030:5. The PR body (`--summary`) lists, in order: "Acceptance criteria not met", deferred HIGHs, then all other deferred findings; `--deferred` lists them too. Agents merge only through merge.mjs, which follows the review gate's merge policy (default off: the operator merges). Never gh pr merge, auto-merge or the merge button. The merge is the approval, and the operator can send the branch back.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:1031:   **Merge tier** (`--summary` only reports it; merge.mjs below acts on it). Each close logs a tier, and `--summary` prints `Merge tier: LOW|MEDIUM|HIGH (<reasons>). Policy <policy>: would merge | needs a human`. HIGH: a riskPaths or `merge.alwaysHuman` file (plus `.github/workflows/**` and `package.json`), a deferred HIGH or protected finding, a protected pre-existing finding, a security pass the config needs that no review ran, a partial close or `--no-tests`; LOW: nothing deferred and only docs, tests or static files changed; MEDIUM otherwise.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:1035:6. Agents never re-rate a finding (severity and score fields stay refused), defer in the first round, edit, re-log or forge review records, or ask the operator to run `--waive` or `REVIEW_GATE_OVERRIDE`.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:1040:11. Show the numbers: every finding shown to the operator carries its scores, e.g. `LOW 6/100 (likelihood 2/10 × impact 3/10)`, or `unscored`, never just the word. The report, `--summary` and `--deferred` print them that way.
builds/agent-command/slice.out:1050:Every PR body must include a section titled exactly **Deferred findings**, using the deferred and filed findings in --summary (or `none` when empty). When chasing a bug, first check `node <review.mjs> --deferred` and merged PR descriptions with `gh pr list --state merged --search "Deferred findings in:body"`. Resolve <review.mjs> repo-first, with the shared runtime fallback above. `--deferred` lists this repo by repoId, newest first; add `--all` for every repo. The trigger is the input or call path named in the issue.
