builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:17:- f1 MEDIUM lib/agent.js:84 — Transcript discovery ignores alternate harness homes. ACS’s generated shell exports CODEX_HOME for private Codex seats, but discovery always searches ~/.codex. A completed worker therefore returns timeout with transcriptMissing, and answer fails. GROK_HOME has the same defect. Both cases reproduced using in-memory transcripts.; Trigger: Open a Codex worker using an ACS private account home, or a Grok worker with GROK_HOME configured, then run wait or answer.; also at lib/agent.js:84, lib/agent.js:93
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:18:- f2 MEDIUM lib/agent.js:312 — A follow-up typed directly into the worker pane remains classified as done because turnComplete advances only through agent send. Reproduction returned done while the new turn had an open tool, then force-closed the active worker after 30 minutes. This also excludes active work from the four-worker limit and disables its stall checks.; Trigger: After a worker finishes, type and submit a correction directly in its cmux pane, then invoke wait or list.; also at lib/agent.js:312, lib/agent.js:277
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:19:- f3 MEDIUM lib/agent.js:274 — Resolved permission requests remain stuck until the entire turn completes. The historical PermissionRequest remains selected even after its matching tool result appears. Reproduction still returned stuck while a subsequent command exceeded command-after, so the obsolete permission event masks later faults.; Trigger: Approve a worker’s permission dialog in its pane, then wait again while the same turn continues executing tools.
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:64:index 580c724..15f11e4 100644
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:116:+## f1 MEDIUM lib/agent.js:84
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:123:+## f2 MEDIUM lib/agent.js:312
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:130:+## f3 MEDIUM lib/agent.js:274
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:154:+- f1: honour CODEX_HOME and GROK_HOME (and CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR for Claude) when locating transcripts, using the worker's launch environment.
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:155:+- f2: a new turn started in the transcript (typed by the user in the pane) counts as running work, whatever its origin.
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:156:+- f3: a permission request is resolved once its tool result (or a later turn event) appears.
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:173:+| f1 MEDIUM | Fixed (`agent-review-fixes.mjs`, case `f1`): launch records the effective harness home, discovery uses that record, and the launcher preserves explicit CODEX_HOME / CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR / GROK_HOME overrides. Unset overrides remain unset. Wait/answer succeed in private homes even when the later caller's environment points elsewhere; executing each launcher against a stub verifies the pinned launch environment. |
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:174:+| f2 MEDIUM | Fixed (`agent-review-fixes.mjs`, case `f2`): latest transcript start/end ordering determines whether the worker is running. Directly submitted turns update activity, remain open, occupy the four-worker limit and retain stall/long-command checks. Claude end-turn records take precedence over delayed/duplicate Stop hooks. The regression covers all vendors, manual follow-up completion, idle-close prevention, late Stop hooks and a queued fifth worker. |
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:175:+| f3 MEDIUM | Fixed (`agent-review-fixes.mjs`, case `f3`): permission events retire on matching results, later turns, timestamped subsequent results when no dialog remains, or an observed approval dialog disappearing. Requests without enough resolution evidence remain pending. A new request gets its own age, and unrelated results cannot resolve an identified request. Tests cover later long commands, newly pending requests, dismissed dialogs with a still-open command, and hooks without call ids. |
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:177:+| s2 HIGH | Fixed (`agent-review-fixes.mjs`, case `s2`): send rejects stuck workers with exit 3 and a named stuck state, checking fresh screen/hook state before text, before each Enter, and during submission verification. All three vendors' already-open trust/permission dialogs receive zero text or keys. Dialogs appearing after text or after the first Enter stop retries before a subsequent Enter can answer them. CLI refusal and input-call counts are asserted. |
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:437:++Codex and Grok completion does not rely on cmux Stop hooks.
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:439:++When a known session's transcript remains unresolved after 30 seconds,
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:488:+index 9f8d1da..b3f5f21 100755
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:511:+     # A copy from before roles (or ask-model) existed lacks that file: cmp against a missing file fails, so it is named too.
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:682:++Add builds/agent-command/rig/run.mjs (not shipped) that, in a scratch git repo it creates under $TMPDIR, uses the real `agent` command to provoke each failure and asserts the right `wait` state within the stated time: (a) a long message to Codex → must be `done`, never silently `unsent`; (b) a Claude worker started with `--permission-mode default` told to run a shell command → `stuck` within 15s; (c) a worker told to run `sleep 600` with `--stall-after 30` → `stalled` within 45s (b and c use a test-only `--launch-extra` / env hook you add for the rig, documented as test-only); (d) a launcher pointed at a missing binary → `no_start` within 75s; (e) a worker ending with `QUESTION: …` → `done` with question. It prints a table: scenario, harness, expected, got, seconds, pass/fail, and closes every pane it opened. Do not run it — the orchestrator will.
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:695:++- long follow-up (codex) expected done, got no_start 61.5s · permission (claude) expected stuck, got timeout 15.3s · silent turn (claude) expected stalled, got timeout 45.4s · missing binary passed · question (codex) expected done, got no_start.
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:696:++Root cause, confirmed by opening Sol and Opus by hand in a brand-new git repo: both CLIs stop on a folder-trust dialog before any session or hook exists ("Trust this folder? 1. Trust and continue / 2. Quit" for Codex; "No, exit / Yes, I trust this folder" for Claude). `wait` cannot see it from transcripts or cmux events, so it reports no_start/timeout. Real worktrees of a trusted repo don't prompt (trust follows the repo); brand-new repos and untrusted folders do. Also observed earlier: Codex writes nothing to its rollout while it reasons, so a long think looked like `stalled` (false alarm).
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:702:++4. The permission scenario: confirm why Claude reported `timeout` at 15s rather than `stuck` even in the earlier pane tests the PermissionRequest hook arrived ~4s in; check whether the hook needs the session to exist (trust dialog first) — after fix 3 it should pass; if not, find out and fix.
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:722:++Rig now (orchestrator ran it): long follow-up done 16.1s PASS · permission stuck 5.4s PASS · missing binary no_start 60.6s PASS · question done 7.1s PASS · fresh folder trust stuck 3.6s PASS · silent running turn expected stalled, got timeout 45.3s FAIL.
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:763:++- Done detection (cmux Stop events are dropped for Codex under load; Grok never sends Stop): Codex → `task_complete` lines in ~/.codex/sessions/Y/M/D/rollout-*.jsonl whose first line's payload.cwd matches (track the specific rollout by session id once known); Claude → agent.hook.Stop for the surface in ~/.cmuxterm/events.jsonl (also usable: the session transcript ~/.claude/projects/<cwd with / → ->/<session>.jsonl); Grok → `turn_ended` in ~/.grok/sessions/<enc cwd>/<id>/events.jsonl.
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:764:++- Stuck detection: agent.hook.PermissionRequest (and Notification not within ~60s after a finished turn, which is just the idle notice) for that surface_id.
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:851:++- **Interactive panes can stall** on a permission prompt nobody sees. Launch with explicit sandbox and approval flags, and watch for `agent.hook.Notification`.
