builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:3793:{"findings":[{"issue":"Incomplete s2 repair: an unresolved approval can still receive Enter. When a parallel command has exceeded 600 seconds, refresh selects long_command ahead of a newer PermissionRequest. Claude's actual file-edit question, “Do you want to make this edit to …?”, also escapes approvalDialog. Consequently this check permits sending into the affirmative approval selection. An in-memory reproduction recorded both text and Enter reaching the pending dialog, allowing an unapproved edit outside the workspace.","file":"lib/agent.js","line":"524","quote":"  const dialog = approvalDialog(w, screen(w));\n  if (result.state === 'stuck' || dialog) {","suggestion":"Reject unresolved approval requests independently of the earliest reported fault, and recognize the harness's file-edit approval dialogs.","likelihood":3,"impact":10,"protected":true,"trigger":"A Claude worker has a parallel command open for over 600 seconds and a newer file-edit approval pending; call agent send while Yes is selected.","preExisting":false,"instances":null},{"issue":"New regression: ordinary answer text is mistaken for an approval dialog. A completed answer containing a standalone “Would you like to proceed?” matches this detector without any choices or permission hook. Reproduction returned done from wait, then exit 3 from send, preventing the follow-up while that answer remains visible.","file":"lib/agent.js","line":"264","quote":"  return index < 0 ? null : { kind: 'permission', prompt: rows.slice(index).join('\\n').trim() };","suggestion":"Require evidence of an active approval interface, including its choices, instead of matching question text alone.","likelihood":3,"impact":5,"protected":false,"trigger":"A worker finishes with a multiline answer containing “Would you like to proceed?”; send it a follow-up using agent send.","preExisting":false,"instances":null}]}
builds/agent-command/fixcheck.out:3794:✓ Fix check fix-1794855207521-8ef027 recorded at 600fb4f8e48cb9bcecde700359731fdf19669837: 2 finding(s)
builds/agent-command/review.out:246:+Codex and Grok completion does not rely on cmux Stop hooks.
builds/agent-command/review.out:248:+When a known session's transcript remains unresolved after 30 seconds,
builds/agent-command/review.out:297:index 9f8d1da..b3f5f21 100755
builds/agent-command/review.out:320:     # A copy from before roles (or ask-model) existed lacks that file: cmp against a missing file fails, so it is named too.
builds/agent-command/review.out:491:+Add builds/agent-command/rig/run.mjs (not shipped) that, in a scratch git repo it creates under $TMPDIR, uses the real `agent` command to provoke each failure and asserts the right `wait` state within the stated time: (a) a long message to Codex → must be `done`, never silently `unsent`; (b) a Claude worker started with `--permission-mode default` told to run a shell command → `stuck` within 15s; (c) a worker told to run `sleep 600` with `--stall-after 30` → `stalled` within 45s (b and c use a test-only `--launch-extra` / env hook you add for the rig, documented as test-only); (d) a launcher pointed at a missing binary → `no_start` within 75s; (e) a worker ending with `QUESTION: …` → `done` with question. It prints a table: scenario, harness, expected, got, seconds, pass/fail, and closes every pane it opened. Do not run it — the orchestrator will.
builds/agent-command/review.out:504:+- long follow-up (codex) expected done, got no_start 61.5s · permission (claude) expected stuck, got timeout 15.3s · silent turn (claude) expected stalled, got timeout 45.4s · missing binary passed · question (codex) expected done, got no_start.
builds/agent-command/review.out:505:+Root cause, confirmed by opening Sol and Opus by hand in a brand-new git repo: both CLIs stop on a folder-trust dialog before any session or hook exists ("Trust this folder? 1. Trust and continue / 2. Quit" for Codex; "No, exit / Yes, I trust this folder" for Claude). `wait` cannot see it from transcripts or cmux events, so it reports no_start/timeout. Real worktrees of a trusted repo don't prompt (trust follows the repo); brand-new repos and untrusted folders do. Also observed earlier: Codex writes nothing to its rollout while it reasons, so a long think looked like `stalled` (false alarm).
builds/agent-command/review.out:511:+4. The permission scenario: confirm why Claude reported `timeout` at 15s rather than `stuck` even in the earlier pane tests the PermissionRequest hook arrived ~4s in; check whether the hook needs the session to exist (trust dialog first) — after fix 3 it should pass; if not, find out and fix.
builds/agent-command/review.out:531:+Rig now (orchestrator ran it): long follow-up done 16.1s PASS · permission stuck 5.4s PASS · missing binary no_start 60.6s PASS · question done 7.1s PASS · fresh folder trust stuck 3.6s PASS · silent running turn expected stalled, got timeout 45.3s FAIL.
builds/agent-command/review.out:572:+- Done detection (cmux Stop events are dropped for Codex under load; Grok never sends Stop): Codex → `task_complete` lines in ~/.codex/sessions/Y/M/D/rollout-*.jsonl whose first line's payload.cwd matches (track the specific rollout by session id once known); Claude → agent.hook.Stop for the surface in ~/.cmuxterm/events.jsonl (also usable: the session transcript ~/.claude/projects/<cwd with / → ->/<session>.jsonl); Grok → `turn_ended` in ~/.grok/sessions/<enc cwd>/<id>/events.jsonl.
builds/agent-command/review.out:573:+- Stuck detection: agent.hook.PermissionRequest (and Notification not within ~60s after a finished turn, which is just the idle notice) for that surface_id.
builds/agent-command/review.out:660:+- **Interactive panes can stall** on a permission prompt nobody sees. Launch with explicit sandbox and approval flags, and watch for `agent.hook.Notification`.
