/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:40:Location: `~/.grok/config.toml`. If the file is missing, Grok uses its built-in defaults, so you only need to set the values you want to override.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:124:Interactive `grok` uses `[agent]` in `config.toml` when you do not pass `--plan`, `--ask-user`, or `--agent-profile`:
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:156:You can also override this with `GROK_DEFAULT_SELECTED_PERMISSION`, which is handy for headless or agent test runs that shouldn't mutate `config.toml`. Precedence: env var → `config.toml` → `allow_once`.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:167:Toggle it at runtime with `/vim-mode`, set it with `/vim-mode nav` or `/vim-mode off`, or pick it from `/settings` → **Vim keys**. Grok writes the change to `[keys] vim` immediately and applies it to every future pager session, including new agents and subagents in the same process. There's no per-session override — `config.toml` is the source of truth on next launch. An older `[ui] vim_mode = true` still reads as `"nav"`: a valid `[keys] vim` wins within the same file (an unrecognized value counts as unset), and across files the higher layer wins, so your `config.toml` beats `managed_config.toml`. `grok doctor` and `/doctor` flag an older `vim_mode` or a retired `simple_mode` line in your `config.toml`.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:171:`[ui] screen_mode` is the **default render mode** for plain `grok` launches. Set it from `/settings` → **Default screen mode** (restart required) or edit `config.toml` by hand — both write the file. CLI flags (`--minimal` / `--fullscreen`) and slash commands (`/minimal` / `/fullscreen`) are session-scoped and do **not** write this key; after a slash switch, the reverse command returns you for that session only.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:205:Each setting also has an environment-variable override, applied on first load only (again, handy for headless / test runs): `GROK_SCROLL_SPEED`, `GROK_SCROLL_MODE`, `GROK_INVERT_SCROLL` (`1`/`true`/`0`/`false`), and `GROK_SCROLL_LINES`. Precedence: env var → `config.toml` → default. Unrecognized values fall back to the default, and out-of-range numbers clamp.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:232:`[toolset.web_search]` constrains the `web_search` tool's domains — the allowlist/blocklist the search itself runs under (not a post-filter). `allowed_domains` and `excluded_domains` are **mutually exclusive**; if you set both, the allowlist wins and the blocklist is dropped with a warning. An empty or absent list is unbounded. This applies to both the backend-hosted search (models with server-side search) and the client-side fallback. A configured policy is **authoritative**: it cannot be bypassed by the model — the model's own per-call `allowed_domains` is ignored whenever you have set `allowed_domains` or `excluded_domains` here (so a blocklist is a real block). The model's per-call allowlist only applies when you have configured nothing. Resolution: requirements → user `config.toml` → managed → default (unset). Config is read at session start, so edit it before starting a session — changes don't apply mid-session.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:234:`[toolset.ask_user_question]` is honored across **requirements.toml**, **managed config**, and your user **`config.toml`**. Precedence: requirements → env (`GROK_ASK_USER_QUESTION_TIMEOUT_ENABLED` / `GROK_ASK_USER_QUESTION_TIMEOUT_SECS`) → user config → managed → defaults. Set `timeout_enabled = false` in your user config to disable the automatic questionnaire timeout for yourself; `timeout_secs` must be a positive integer. You can also toggle `timeout_enabled` from `/settings` → **Ask-Question timeout** (under Agent & Approval); changes apply to newly started sessions.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:309:MCP servers can also be set per-project in `.grok/config.toml`. Project-scoped config contributes `[mcp_servers]`, `[plugins]`, and `[permission]` rules; every other section loads only from `~/.grok/config.toml`.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:311:Priority for `[mcp_servers]` and `[plugins]`: `.grok/config.toml` (current dir) > `<repo-root>/.grok/config.toml` > `~/.grok/config.toml`, except that an MCP server whose transport or `enabled = false` comes from `requirements.toml` / MDM keeps the org setting. `[permission]` rules aren't overridden by priority — they merge across all files with `deny` > `ask` > `allow` (see [22-permissions-and-safety.md](22-permissions-and-safety.md)).
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:377:hooks = true      # scan ~/.cursor/hooks.json and <cwd>/.cursor/hooks.json
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:385:hooks = true      # scan ~/.claude/settings.json for hooks
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:396:Each cell can be set via environment variable or `config.toml`; the [`compat` rows of the config reference](26-config-reference.md#compat) list the names. Resolution: env var > config.toml > default (on).
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:412:`[hints]` is read from the **effective config merge**, with the usual precedence: system managed → user `managed_config.toml` → user `config.toml` → user `requirements.toml` → system `requirements.toml`, higher layers winning. The TUI only ever **writes** these to your user `~/.grok/config.toml`.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:509:Run your own commands when events fire. Hooks receive `$GROK_EVENT`, `$GROK_MESSAGE`, and `$GROK_SESSION_ID` in the environment. Each hook has its own `events` and `only_unfocused` filters. The `condition` and `events` above do not apply to hooks. Hooks are read only from your own `config.toml`, never from managed config, overlays or a project's `.grok`, and they do not run under `allow_managed_hooks_only`.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:570:  - Some builds also send content-free usage metrics (session, turn, token, and tool-call counts and timings). They're on by default. `DISABLE_TELEMETRY=1` turns them off, as does turning telemetry off: `false` in a config file or `GROK_TELEMETRY_ENABLED`, a `requirements.toml` / MDM pin, or `DISABLE_TELEMETRY` in managed settings. A `requirements.toml`, `managed-settings.json`, or config file that Grok can't read also keeps them off. Grok reads these switches when it starts, so a change applies the next time you start Grok.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:608:process env (destination lock). `managed_config.toml` does not. There is no
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:818:| `GROK_DISABLE_EXPERIMENTS` | Turns every `[[experiments]]` entry off when set to any value except `0`, `false`, `no` or `off`. Each entry is `disabled (env_opt_out)` in `grok inspect` and applies nothing, so an experiment can't change your channel or settings. Experiments you've left stay left, and when you unset the variable, the entries apply again. Configuration equivalent: `[experiments] enabled = false` in any configuration file (`config.toml`, either `managed_config.toml`, a requirements file or the MDM payload); any `enabled = false` turns experiments off, and `enabled = true` doesn't override it. | 1.0.70 or later |
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:854:| `~/.grok/config.toml` | Main configuration file |
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:864:| `.grok/config.toml` | Project-scoped MCP servers, plugins, and permission rules |
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:871:### How Grok saves `config.toml`
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:873:Writes to **`~/.grok/config.toml`** (`/settings`, `/vim-mode`, and other user-config saves) follow a leaf symlink. The atomic rename writes the referent (a file in your dotfiles repo). The link stays a link. If the link is dangling, the write creates the referent as a regular file.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:875:Writes to a **project** `.grok/config.toml` (MCP / plugin / permission edits) **replace** a leaf symlink with a regular file. That keeps a later save from following the link out of the repository.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:877:A user `config.toml` that cannot be parsed is not overwritten. Fix the syntax (or restore a backup) and save again.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:887:| `.grok/config.toml` | MCP servers, plugins, permission rules, and the `[mcp] max_output_bytes` tool-result cap (other sections load only from `~/.grok/config.toml`) |
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/26-config-reference.md:3:This file ships with the CLI and is extracted to `~/.grok/docs/user-guide/26-config-reference.md` on launch. It is the complete field list for `config.toml`, `managed_config.toml`, and `requirements.toml`. For conceptual guidance see [05-configuration.md](05-configuration.md).

