/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/10-hooks.md:84:**Trusting a project**: The first time you open a project with hooks, you must trust it before its project hooks will run; until then they are silently skipped. Grant trust by running `/hooks-trust` (or launching with `--trust`); the decision is recorded in the unified folder-trust store (`~/.grok/trusted_folders.toml`), the same gate that governs repo-local MCP/LSP servers. As in Claude Code, a folder that isn't trusted also holds back your own hooks: those in `~/.grok/hooks/`, `hooks-paths`, `~/.grok/config.toml`, an unsigned `~/.grok/requirements.toml`, `~/.claude/settings.json` and `~/.cursor/hooks.json`, your agents' frontmatter `hooks:`, and your plugins' hooks. While you have such a hook, folder trust asks in every folder but your home directory and filesystem or drive roots, which count as trusted. Only your organization's `managed_config.toml` and `requirements.toml` hooks run before you trust a folder. A headless run, such as `grok -p`, counts the folder as trusted for your own hooks, but project hooks and a project agent's own hooks still need the folder trusted. In that run, a command in your `~/.claude` settings or an agent's `hooks:` that is only a relative path, such as `.claude/hooks/check.sh`, fails with a message saying the folder isn't trusted, while one with arguments or shell syntax still runs. This prevents untrusted repos from running arbitrary code.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/10-hooks.md:92:Events fire at three cadences: once per session (`SessionStart`, `SessionEnd`), once per turn (`UserPromptSubmit`, `Stop`, `StopFailure`), and on every tool call inside the turn (`PreToolUse`, `PostToolUse`, `PostToolUseFailure`).
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/10-hooks.md:96:| `SessionStart` | A session starts, or Grok reopens a saved one. Does not fire for a subagent's own session. Compaction fires only `PreCompact` and `PostCompact`, never `SessionStart`. | No |
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/10-hooks.md:123:Grok runs the matcher groups in `~/.cursor/hooks.json` and `.cursor/hooks.json`.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/10-hooks.md:132:Every other hook source (Claude settings, plugin `hooks.json`, Grok hook files and `config.toml`) reads only matcher groups.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/10-hooks.md:139:| `sessionStart`, `sessionEnd` | `SessionStart`, `SessionEnd` |
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/10-hooks.md:181:- **matcher** (optional): A name, a list of names, or a regular expression. It picks the invocations that trigger the hook (see [Matcher Syntax](#matcher-syntax)). What it tests depends on the event: the tool name on tool events (`PreToolUse`, `PostToolUse`, `PostToolUseFailure`, `PermissionDenied`), the notification type on `Notification`, the subagent type on `SubagentStart`/`SubagentStop` (e.g. `explore`), the start source on `SessionStart` (`new` for a fresh session, including one from `/new` or `/clear`, or `load` for a saved session reopened with `--resume`, `--continue`, or the `/resume` picker; Claude Code's `startup`, `resume`, `clear`, and `compact` never match), the end reason on `SessionEnd`, the compaction trigger on `PreCompact`/`PostCompact` (`manual` or `auto`), the error type on `StopFailure` (`rate_limit`, `authentication_failed`, `invalid_request`, `server_error`, `max_output_tokens`, or `unknown`), and the reason on `StopCancelled` (`user_interrupt`, `permission_rejected`, `permission_cancelled`, `max_turns`, `no_progress`, `hook_stopped`, or `unknown`). A matcher on `Stop` or `UserPromptSubmit` is ignored with a warning (those events always fire). An empty or omitted matcher matches everything. A finish-thinking chime should set `matcher` to `idle_prompt` on `Notification` (any turn end, then sustained idle). `permission_prompt` fires only when a permission UI is actually waiting. The matcher tests the real tool name. MCP calls routed through the internal `use_tool` / Cursor `CallMcpTool` dispatchers appear as the qualified `server__tool` name (e.g. `linear__save_issue`). Match on that name, not the dispatcher name. A matcher can also use Claude Code's `mcp__server__tool` name.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/10-hooks.md:298:2. **Run the handlers in order.** Handlers in the selected groups run in config order, each receiving the event as JSON on stdin, until one returns `deny` (which stops the chain). Handlers from different sources (global, project, plugin, config) are merged, and a handler that several selected groups name runs once per call, even when their matchers differ. Two handlers are the same when they run the same thing with the same arguments and the same `env`: the same URL, or the same command resolved the same way with the same `args`. A relative command such as `guard.sh` resolves against its own hook file's directory, so `guard.sh` in `~/.grok/hooks/` and in `.grok/hooks/` are two handlers. Every copy loads, and `/hooks` lists each one. When copies differ in `timeout`, the first copy in config order runs with its own timeout, unless a later copy has higher authority (a managed one, for example), which then runs in the first copy's place. When copies differ in `failClosed`, the run fails closed if any copy set it, whichever copy runs. A plugin's or agent's handler is its own, so it never merges with another plugin's or agent's. Every handler sees the model's original tool input; a `PreToolUse` `updatedInput` is applied only after all handlers finish, so one handler cannot see another's rewrite (the last rewrite wins).
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/10-hooks.md:371:- **What is skipped.** Every other hook, at dispatch: `~/.grok/hooks`, every `~/.grok/*.toml` file, project hooks, plugin hooks, agent frontmatter hooks, the Claude and Cursor compatibility files, and the pager's `[[ui.notifications.hooks]]` commands. In `/hooks` they show `[disabled]`, and enabling them is refused ("Hooks outside managed policy are disabled by your organization."); `grok inspect` names the file that set the pin.
/Users/cortega/.grok/docs/user-guide/10-hooks.md:530:  Observe-only events, such as `SessionStart`, `Notification` and `PreCompact`, read only a top-level `systemMessage` from the reply.

