      "issue": "Tool permissions: the Codex launcher makes the whole shared Git common directory writable to the sandboxed worker, including `config` and `hooks/`, not just the data a commit needs. A Codex worker in a linked worktree (run with `-s workspace-write -a never`, so no human check) can then write `<trunk>/.git/hooks/*` or set `core.fsmonitor`, `core.sshCommand`, a local `core.hooksPath` or `reviewgate.enabled` in `<trunk>/.git/config`. Those run or apply outside the sandbox on the next git command by the orchestrator or operator, in the trunk and every worktree. A repo-local `core.hooksPath` overrides the global review-gate hooks, and `reviewgate.enabled` is the gate's opt-in (packs/review-gate/runtime/hooks/global/run-hook:23, project.mjs:28), so this also lets a worker switch the push gate off. Harmed principal: the operator. Attacker: whoever can steer the worker through content it reads (repo files, dependency docs, tool output), or a worker that simply misbehaves. Second problem at the same line: the directory comes from repo-controlled metadata with no check. tests/agent-cli.mjs:172-177 shows a hand-written `.git` file plus a `commondir` file is enough for git to report a path, so a crafted folder (for example an extracted archive) can point `--add-dir` at any other repository's `.git` on the machine. Not demonstrated end to end: exploitation needs a steered worker, which I did not reproduce.",
